                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "0b9686a3-d37f-4f41-b35c-c262346f8d09",
                        "entry_id": "722ea3fd-2989-3bf3-9f17-156f3981a68c",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural"
                        ],
                        "form": "scythes"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "470ef108-ff9b-3ef7-b8ea-52adf0181fe1",
                        "entry_id": "722ea3fd-2989-3bf3-9f17-156f3981a68c",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Relatif aux Scythes, un peuple barbare des bords de la mer Noire."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "9dc551f8-a392-4286-be14-1d4d588b49be",
                                "sense_id": "470ef108-ff9b-3ef7-b8ea-52adf0181fe1",
                                "text": "Ce qui est merveilleux, poursuivit le militaire, c’est que personne n’est coupable. Et pourtant, il y avait peut-être là-bas une quinzaine de kilos d’or. Songez que le trésor de Pristchépino contenait vingt-cinq kilos d’or scythe , et cinquante d’argent. C’est un plein sac de devises qui vient de nous passer sous le nez !\nC’est vraiment un amour, pensa Zybine. Il est même au courant de l’or scythe."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "d621dd32-469f-44cd-8eac-1d0af87b9f16",
                                "sense_id": "470ef108-ff9b-3ef7-b8ea-52adf0181fe1",
                                "text": "L’art scythe."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "89aecbbf-b434-4622-8427-02fabc29d4da",
                        "source_entry_id": "722ea3fd-2989-3bf3-9f17-156f3981a68c",
                        "source_sense_id": "470ef108-ff9b-3ef7-b8ea-52adf0181fe1",
                        "target_lang_word": "Scythian",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "Scythian",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "d7602c67-c2be-31c3-a530-d8f25b2de16a",
                                        "entry_id": "e21cb6cf-8ebc-36c8-82e6-8b19f7b7a1bc",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Of or relating to Scythia (a region of Central Eurasia in the classical era) or its inhabitants."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": []
                                    }
                                ]
                            },
                            {
                                "target_word": "Scythian",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "a6847cbd-3aca-3c11-84f0-a421a8518540",
                                        "entry_id": "0f0bdedb-df37-344b-9a5b-0dc9a0b500dd",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "An inhabitant of Scythia."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "95fb2cd1-3d32-4773-891f-b9a8a510721a",
                                                "sense_id": "a6847cbd-3aca-3c11-84f0-a421a8518540",
                                                "text": "1685, John Norris, (translator), The Institution and Life of Cyrus the Great [Cyropaedia] by Xenophon Book I\nHow far he surpassed them all may be felt if we remember that no Scythian, although the Scythians are reckoned by their myriads, has ever succeeded in dominating a foreign nation […]"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            },
                            {
                                "target_word": "Scythian",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "8b4500d6-00e7-3bbd-aa02-dc169e08a659",
                                        "entry_id": "001d4496-db89-3da8-9a2c-fb5d67572071",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "The Eastern Iranian language of Scythians."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": []
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "d4457551-4bc6-33af-9196-d61da5157c85",
                                        "entry_id": "001d4496-db89-3da8-9a2c-fb5d67572071",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "The Proto-Indo-European language."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "Indo-European-studies"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "0368491c-14cd-4734-b044-21ecf7e7eac4",
                                                "sense_id": "d4457551-4bc6-33af-9196-d61da5157c85",
                                                "text": "Scythian was therefore not the same as modern Dutch, but the ancestral language of the Dutch as well as other neighbouring languages."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "8273bddb-7c19-4112-8ca5-530c39bc7dea",
                                                "sense_id": "d4457551-4bc6-33af-9196-d61da5157c85",
                                                "text": "Though Boxhorn was working over a century before Sir William Jones, his ‘Scythian’ is not conceptually far off the later notion of Indo-European, and his choice of term ‘Scythian’ shows that Boxhorn was envisaging a cultural connection between the (northern) languages of the Near East and their Western European brethren, a perspicacious hypothesis that assumend some kind of ethnic drift between the two continents."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "3fad3cfc-2684-4832-8da1-5fc4be133806",
                                                "sense_id": "d4457551-4bc6-33af-9196-d61da5157c85",
                                                "text": "From 1647 onwards, in several of his writings, Boxhorn elaborated the idea that Scythian was the original mother language of Persian, Greek, Latin, the Germanic languages, Turkish, Welsh, Lithuanian, Russian and Latvian (cf. Boxhorn 1647)."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
